renderer: a function from one file to nodes. The general idea behind shelf,
page and diff; python's page is the case that made it plain.

  file --renderer--> nodes --> engine
                     details#id > summary(heading, edge...) + body

  The node is the contract (medium/page.html, checked by medium/page/run.mjs).
  The engine is keys, folds, find, address. It does not know the kind. A
  renderer fills heading, edge and body and nothing else.

Where a renderer runs is a detail, not a design:

  kind        renderer            runs        why there
  .md  .js    shelf outline       the page    text only; sections() / defs()
  .diff       shelf outline       the page    text only
  .py         page/py             terminal    needs ast; the page cannot
  diff.md     page/diff           terminal    needs ast and two trees
  todo folder page/todo           terminal    a typed state per entry
  a new kind  a new renderer      either      text -> page; parse -> terminal

Rule: a kind that needs a parser gets a generator and its own page; a kind that
is only text stays in the shelf's outline. The kind picks the renderer by file
extension, so a new kind never changes the shelf or the engine.

Two views of every file: rendered and source
  The renderer's page is the rendered view; the file as it is on disk is the
  source view. A key flips between them on the same place, and the node's id
  is what keeps the place (rendered Field.broken_constraint <-> its line range
  in the source, 206..225).

    rendered   renderer(file) -> nodes        what it means, folded
    source     the file, line numbers        what it says, whole
       flip    same id, same cursor

  Source is never lost: the rendered body already holds the code as written
  (todo/studio/refactor, "body"), so rendered is a view over source, not a copy
  of it. A kind with no renderer shows source only. That is what the shelf does
  today for any file it does not know.
  Open: where the flip key lives (the shelf's keys, page.html's engine, or
  both); and whether a source view needs ids at all, or only line ranges.

Three ideas, no more
  node       details#id > summary + body. The one contract.
  renderer   a source in, nodes out: page/py, page/diff, page/todo.
  page       page.html, the one reader of nodes. Keys, folds, find, the box and
             the trail (title, then each node down to the one being read, a
             link back out of each). It knows nothing of the source.

  A layout is a rule on the page, not another page: cards in a column, or
  tiles in a grid (main.tiles). The engine is the same; the grid only changes
  which key is a row. page/todo makes a folder's entries tiles, in the order of
  their state, the data typed: state, title, after.

  The node is a list, so a note or a folder reads into one and saves as json:
  programs ask it, the studio only adds renderers.

One list, every page
  The aim: each html is a view of a list, never the source. A note, a folder
  and a page's main all read to the same list, (node title leaf...), and a
  view is a renderer over it.
  source    text a person writes: indent is depth, "name  text" a row, code
            fenced. A folder is a node, its files the children
  list      the truth. Saved as json, so a program asks it
  view      page.html, the studio, a terminal pane: renderers over the list
  A leaf has a type and a component: para, row, step, code, table, tally.
  A component is one function, type in, html out, its css in page.html. An
  unknown leaf passes through as it is; a one-off figure is a local leaf, as
  local css is. No framework: a new type is a new function.
  Code first: a block is a value, not a picture of one. It has a caption,
  a kind, and later a name, and that is what lets the page do something
  with it.
    now      kept as typed, copy beside it
    later    "name.py" as the caption: a link to the file's own line
    later    a block that is data (rows, json) can be checked or sorted
  Nothing here asks the writer to leave plain text: every later row is
  opt-in by a caption.
  Move a page: extract its main's nodes to the list, render it back, run.mjs
  both. If they agree the page is source-free; if not, the gap is a leaf type
  or a local leaf. One page at a time, the old html kept until then.
  Order: reader (text to list, --json), extractor (html to list), round trip
  on design.html, then the rest.
  The lisp notebook (~/Data/today/lisp-notebook.txt) is this, not a second
  system. A note is a file, a subject a folder, both nodes. Tags are head
  keys. today/ and tagged/<tag>/ stop being hand-made symlinks and become
  queries over the list, as that note says it wants. Pollen's "a word is a
  function" is the component: type in, html out. Lisp stays the list's form;
  evaluating a block is a later leaf type, not a prerequisite.

What it buys
- one checker for all: node medium/page/run.mjs on any page a renderer made
- a better renderer replaces a worse one without touching the rest (the
  shelf's plain python rows, then page/py's ast; the node does not change)
- the same renderer feeds a page, a terminal pane or an app: the app case,
  `todo/studio/shelf:/If the shelf becomes an app/`, changes who runs it,
  not what it makes

Open
- the shelf opening a .py: link to the made page, or load its nodes in place.
  Start with the link; embed if the keys are missed
- python is drawn twice now that page/py exists: the shelf's plain rows (step
  3's def-line heading) and the made page. The rows are the quick outline; they
  grow no rail
- a caption by ":" at the end of a line: too clever for prose?
- rows by two spaces: a typo makes a table
